Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"A variety of factors could cause us to be incontinent, or lack self-control":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A multitude of factors may prevent us from being able to act in a continent manner.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why incontinence exists.</p><h4>Incontinence</h4><p>Incontinence is the lack of self-control, and it regards pleasure and pain (specifically, an excess of one or the other).</p><ul><li>It is acting wrongly beyond one’s own control (where as licentiousness is willed wrongness of action).</li><li>It is something to avoid and to condemn.</li></ul><p>Additionally, one can be incontinent contingently, i.e., incontinent regarding a specific thing. If this is the case, such a person is not generally “incontinent,” but simply specifically incontinent about something. In fact, we can judge different particular incontinences based on how bad they are. For example:</p><ul><li>Incontinence of temper is not as bad as incontinence of desire, since temper is rational (though exaggerated), and it is not cultivated or indulgent, and it is “less deliberate.”</li></ul><p>There are also two types of incontinence:</p><ol><li>Impetuosity: failure to deliberate –&gt; acting in accordance with one’s desires.</li><li>Weakness: successful deliberation –&gt; but failure to abide by the decision of the deliberation.</li></ol><p>It is easier to cure the former (if they are not also weak), but the latter is difficult to change. Additionally, it easier to change habits than natures.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Some factors that may cause incontinence include:</p><ul><li>Having knowledge but not using it.</li><li>Having a mistake in applying the Practical Syllogism.<ul><li>This may be because there was an error during the deliberation of the syllogism, which causes the wrong action to be performed, or</li><li>Desire pushed us to abide by the incorrect syllogism.</li></ul></li><li>By mentally incapacitated or just in a circumstance where continence or use of knowledge is impossible.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","American democracy is too populist":"<h5>Summary</h5><p>American democracy is too populist, focusing too much on “democracy,” and not enough on constitutional liberalism and the overall wellbeing of the nation.</p><h5>Significance</h5><p>This idea presents an interesting critique of American democracy that presents its “democracticness” in a more nuanced view.</p><h4>Rising Populism</h4><p>American politicians are catering to the masses too much, and ignoring the actual needs of the nation and constitutional liberalism.</p>","Christ carrying the Cross":"<h4>Notes</h4><p>Again, evidence for El Greco’s supreme skill as a portraitist.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Portrait of Jerónimo de Cevallos\">Portrait of Jerónimo de Cevallos</a><ul><li>Another portrait by El Greco.</li></ul></li></ul>","Democracies are doomed to mediocrity since they are ultimately, on average, subject to mediocre people":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Democracies are doomed to always be mediocre since the people in power are, in the end, selected by the average (mediocre) people.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is part of Mills argument for tolerance, since in doing so, more people of “strong character” can be formed and thus bring the society out of mediocrity.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"American democracy is too populist\">American democracy is too populist</a><ul><li>The reason that populism is bad is because the people who populists appeal to are merely mediocre, and thus don’t truly understand what is good for themselves or the country.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding\">Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding</a><ul><li>Again, this could because mediocre people are choosing the people who run the government.</li></ul></li></ul>","Divided leadership compromises the effectiveness of an army":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This is often one of the major problems that can compromise an army, since divided leadership means that the army is unable to act as a coordinated whole.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This was one of the major factors that cause military failures.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>Examples of this can be found all through out the Second Punic war, especially at the battles of the Trebia, Trasimene, and Cannae.</p><h4>References</h4>","Form outlives function":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>It is often the case that while some object or process originally possessed an actual, practical purpose, eventually, it becomes ritualized.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is something that seems to happen across cultures and institutions, and is a good heuristic when analyzing the nature of cultural processes or the process by which institutions develop.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>Many of the instruments that were developed for practical purposes, such as the <em>yue</em> or the <em>ding</em>, which began as a practical food storage unit, gradually morphed into objects with greater/ritual significance.<ul><li>Other examples of this in Chinese culture include the <em>ruyi</em> (which began as a back-scratcher) and the fan, which increasingly became more ornate.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure\">When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure</a><ul><li>It seems that often times, objects that were originally meant to be used later becomes abstracted into a “target,” which leads to the end of its nature as a good/practice thing/process.</li></ul></li></ul>","France’s strict regulation, high worker benefits, and high pensions are crippling the economy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>France’s system of strict regulation on business (high working standards), and extremely generous pensions are crippling the French economy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This poses an existential threat to the French state.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>France has the 3rd highest tax wedge in the OECD on an average single worker, at 47.2%.</li><li>It is very difficult to fire people.<ul><li>This means that firms are incentivized to hire less people, leading to a high youth unemployment rate (since established, older workers cannot be fire, and new workers won’t be hired since they can’t be fired).</li><li>They have consistently high unemployment rates as a result, being around 7% (across the general population as well, not just youth).</li></ul></li><li>The 35 hour work week led to higher labor turnover rates, since labor costs were essentially higher.</li><li>France has an exceedingly low age of retirement (62), and as the ratio of retiree to worker increases, it is going to get more and more difficult to fund the pension program.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Legal regulation is not context sensitive\">Legal regulation is not context sensitive</a><ul><li>This is an example of this concept in practice.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy\">Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy</a><ul><li>If the French deregulated, their economy would improve.</li></ul></li></ul>","LLMs are not good at counting and manipulating individual characters":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to the tokenization mechanism behind LLMs, they are not able to complexly manipulate individual characters in strings of text, nor are they good at counting.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why although LLMs are often able to answer high level problems, they often struggle with simple operations such as counting or spelling.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Due the nature of tokenization, where words/strings of characters are often represented as one token (so individual manipulation of characters is difficult for the LLM to understand), it is difficult for the model to be able to count or spell well.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"LLM hallucinations are &#x27;shameless guesses,&#x27; not &#x27;hallucinations&#x27;\">LLM hallucinations are 'shameless guesses,' not 'hallucinations'</a><ul><li>So the result of tasks involving those described in this note can be categorized as such.</li></ul></li></ul>","Merit is contextual and internal, so it cannot be read off outcomes":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Whether someone is meritorious depends on the context that defines the relevant merit, and merit is a property of the person rather than of what happens to them, so outcomes are at best defeasible evidence of it.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the concession that makes meritocracy defensible in principle and unworkable in practice, since the only measure available to an institution is the outcome.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Belief in meritocracy makes people behave more unfairly\">Belief in meritocracy makes people behave more unfairly</a><ul><li>What happens when outcomes are treated as direct evidence of merit anyway.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure\">When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure</a><ul><li>The mechanism by which a proxy for merit degrades once it is used to allocate.</li></ul></li></ul>","Of all the arts, music is most able to improve the character of the soul":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Compared to all the others arts, music is the best able to bring about different dispositions of character, and thus help people learn more about these dispositions, ultimately allowing them to achieve virtue through this knowledge.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the reason Aristotle then argues that music should be part of the mandatory education.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>His premise is that in rhythms and tunes there are likenesses particularly close to the genuine natures of anger and gentleness, courage and moderation, and their opposites. Then the explicit contrast: no likeness of characters is present in the other perceptible things. In touch and taste there is none, and in visible things it is present only slightly. Figures and colors are not likenesses of characters but only indications of them, and only as character is manifested by the body under passion. Whereas in tunes by themselves there are imitations of characters.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>It seems that in the modern day, film would also be considered virtue-inducing?</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Music can influence your confidence\">Music can influence your confidence</a><ul><li>It seems that modern research somewhat backs this up?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a><ul><li>Maybe music can help with this process.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"People should be trained in the arts so they have refined aesthetic palates\">People should be trained in the arts so they have refined aesthetic palates</a><ul><li>This note is the reason Aristotle argues that people should be trained in music.</li></ul></li></ul>","One cannot voluntarily choose to lose one’s freedom":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>We cannot voluntarily choose to lose our freedom.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>It’s unclear why Mill makes this point, this point being something that is relatively weak as well.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>This note seems obviously false. There does not seem to be a good reason that one cannot voluntarily give up one’s freedom.</p><h4>References</h4>","One’s initial moral sensitivity is irrelevant to whether one ought to be considered morally responsible":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One’s initial moral outlook is irrelevant to how one should be assigned moral responsibility.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a point of clarification that Aristotle makes when he defends against the objection that some people are just naturally less able to be moral than others.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Aristotle argues that if we somehow absolve some people of moral responsibility since they are less able to perform moral actions due to some innate factor, it would be like saying that people who do act virtuously are somehow just innately predisposed to act in the same way and thus should not be praised. This is clearly false.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An act is voluntary when an agent chooses to engage in it\">An act is voluntary when an agent chooses to engage in it</a><ul><li>Acting virtuously is always virtuous, and so someone’s initial conditions when it comes to their ability to act morally doesn’t actually matter to whether they ought to be held morally responsible.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a><ul><li>In order for one to become more virtuous if they do not begin as being virtuous, they have to continuously act virtuously.</li></ul></li></ul>","Pareto improvement does not take into account the relative strength of utilities or disutilities":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea of a pareto improvement is that it leads to an utility gain while leading to no disutility as a result, at all. An action is not considered a pareto improvement even if it raises utility by $100 \\ utils$, if the action lowers utility by even $1 \\ utils$.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>I think it is a little strange for the concept of pareto improvement to be defined this way, considering many other concepts in economics have a focus on maximizing overall welfare.</p><h4>References</h4>","Persons who are born with low but non-negative utility cannot be said to have been ‘harmed’ in their birth":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that someone’s birth into a situation with any non-negative utility cannot to have been said to have been harmed by society. This is the non-identity problem.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the foremost problems in population ethics.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>A slave who is born with barely positive utility does not have the right to claim that they have been harmed by the people who gave birth to them, even if they knew perfectly well the circumstances into which their child would have been born. This is because their utility, despite being low, is still non-negative, which is better than non-existence.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>I think that this paradox is actually quite weak, for two reasons:</p><ol><li>It relies on the assumption of utilitarianism, which has many flaws.</li><li>It seems to exploit our in ability to fully comprehend the idea of “utility.” It seems that having a non-negative utility in life would, almost as a tautology, make that life worth living, even though intuitively, that might not seem to be the case.</li></ol><h4>References</h4>","Political institutions must constantly change in order to meet the needs of the times":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Political institutions cannot remain the same across time. They must constantly change and keep up with the times, since morals and cultural values change over time, and institutions should match these.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This represents a development in the political theory of the Taoists, who now implicitly accept the existence of a state.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>As opposed to Laozi or Zhuangzi, Guo Xiang argues that political institutions ought to exist, but that they should exist while abiding by the nature of the times, and should not remain stuck in the past. This is supported by his theory of <em>wu-wei</em>, or acting in one’s natural abilities and going with the flow.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Government institutions, once established, are difficult to end\">Government institutions, once established, are difficult to end</a><ul><li>The issue, practically, is that once institutions are established by the government, they are extremely difficult to change.</li></ul></li></ul>","SNAP does not drive up food prices":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>SNAP does not raise food prices, as some on the right have claimed. While typically a subsidized good would lead to increased demand for the product, and thus drive up the cost, this is not the case for SNAP as a result of the way the program is structured.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a misconception that many have who argued against the efficacy of food stamps.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>While typically, subsidies lead to higher prices since they raise demand for something, this is not the case of SNAP. Or, at least, SNAP does not have an effect on the economy that a normal transfer payment would not.  This is because SNAP essentially makes food free to purchase up to $300. So, although households may spend some of the surplus from the $300 on further food items, it may also be spent on other items. In the end, this means that food stamps simply have an effect on the economy that any transfer payment would.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"SNAP does not cause economic growth\">SNAP does not cause economic growth</a></li></ul>","Something that is pleasurable is not necessarily bad":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Many arguments have been put forth for why pleasurable things are bad. Aristotle refutes these arguments.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This note presents some arguments for the moral quality of pleasure, something that is still fiercely debated.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ol><li>Since pleasure is a process by which we reach a desirable state, e.g., we gain pleasure from eating since we don’t want to be hungry, pleasure cannot be an end in and of itself. Therefore, pleasure is not a final good.<ol><li><em>While bodily pleasures involve filling a lack, intellectual pleasures (learning, seeing, smelling ,etc.) involve no pain beforehand. Pleasure is sometime therefore a complete experience in a moment, not a journey to somewhere.</em></li></ol></li><li>Pleasure is a distraction from actually being able to do stuff.<ol><li><em>While foreign pleasures (pleasures unrelated to the task at hand) may be distracting, pleasures can also arise from the activity itself. Such pleasure can actually be beneficial.</em></li></ol></li><li>Children and animals seek pleasure.<ol><li><em>We should not despise what nature dictates, just because nature dictates it.</em></li></ol></li><li>Some pleasures are distasteful, since they are pleasure that is the result of something evil.<ol><li><em>We should be focused on the pleasure experienced by the</em> virtuous <em>person, not the evil/morally corrupt person.</em></li></ol></li></ol><p>There are also some arguments for why pleasure is good:</p><ol><li>It is agreed that pain is (usually) bad, and since the opposite of pain is pleasure, pleasure ought to (usually) be good.</li><li>Since both humans and animals who are rational and irrational respectively seek pleasure, pleasure must be good. Although this does not prove that pleasure is the supreme good.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Temperance is a state whereby lack of pleasure does not pain oneself\">Temperance is a state whereby lack of pleasure does not pain oneself</a><ul><li>A temperance person has the right attitude towards pleasures. They accept the right pleasures when it comes to them, but does not actively seek out base pleasures.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Belgian Revolution occurred because of an especially moving Opera performance":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Belgium Revolution of 1830, which saw the establishment of a United Kingdom of Belgium following its occurrence, was instigated by a particular moving performance of the Opera <em>La muette de Portic</em>.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows the power of music to instill strong emotions in people, such that it can serve as the match that lights a pile of dry sticks on fire, eventually leading to a revolution.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Music can influence your confidence\">Music can influence your confidence</a></li></ul>","The Late Roman Republican period was characterized by a series of increasingly powerful and independent military commanders":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Late Republican period was characterized by generals who essentially had armies that were independent of the control of the Senate, which allowed them to seize power more easily.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This was part of the reason for the fall of the Republic.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The late Roman Republic saw a lot of generals take control of armies since they were personally paying them (as a result of the professionalizing reforms of Marius), which meant that their loyalty was to the particular general, not to the state. This meant that, when the time came, the generals could leverage their personal armies to directly challenge the state.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The fall of the Roman Republic could be partly attributed to the fact that the Senate failed to adequately represent the Plebeians\">The fall of the Roman Republic could be partly attributed to the fact that the Senate failed to adequately represent the Plebeians</a><ul><li>Another reason for the fall of the Republic.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Revenue Equivalence Theorem states that any auction where the highest bidder wins will generate the same expected revenue for the seller":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that no matter the auction format, as long as the auction is one whereby the highest bidder wins, the expected revenue generated through the auction remains the same.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an important law in Auction Theory and serves as the basis for understanding the type of auctions that exist and should be used in particular instances.</p><h4>Conditions</h4><p>There are four conditions for the Revenue Equivalence Theorem to hold true:</p><ol><li><strong>Risk Neutrality</strong>: Bidders are indifferent to risk, only caring about maximizing profit. This means that they will not bid aggressively to ensure that they win.</li><li><strong>Independent Private Values (IPV)</strong>: Each bidder’s valuation of an item is not influenced by the valuation of any other bidder.</li><li><strong>Symmetry</strong>: All bidders draw form the same probability distribution, so that there is no “strong” or “weak” bidder. Every bidder’s values essentially have to have the same range (same probably distribution), and all bidders have to have the same risk profile.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><p>-</p>","The West is in decline":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The West is current in a state of decline, which, although it could take a long time and experience fluctuations, will ultimately progress.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a crucial aspect of Huntington’s thesis in <em>Clash of Civilizations</em>.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The West is lacking in several crucial power resource:</p><ul><li>Territory and population.</li><li>Economic production.</li><li>Military capacity.</li></ul><p>Huntington characterizes this as a relative decline, whereby the rise of the rest is outpacing the growth in power of the West, not directly as a weakening of Western power.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>An example of the decline of the West is there inability to enforce Western morality upon other countries. For instance, in Asia, the West has not been able to successfully enforce a universal application of human rights. This is due to</p><ul><li>The rising influence of Asian economic power, which Western businesses desperately want to work with.</li><li>The fact that Asian nations work together in order oppose what they see as Western encroachment on their sovereignty.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end of the Cold War marked a major change in global security\">The end of the Cold War marked a major change in global security</a><ul><li>This is part of the reason.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The West’s past domination of the world was a result of superior technology, not superior values or religion\">The West’s past domination of the world was a result of superior technology, not superior values or religion</a><ul><li>Perhaps this is the reason why it is so difficult for them to enforce their ideology on others now that the technological differential has disspated?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nations typically Westernize, then modernize, then reject Westernization\">Nations typically Westernize, then modernize, then reject Westernization</a><ul><li>The rejection of Westernization is the result of the decline of the West.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The rejection of Western culture has lead to various ‘affirmations’ of Asian culture\">The rejection of Western culture has lead to various ‘affirmations’ of Asian culture</a><ul><li>A consequence of the decline of the West.</li></ul></li></ul>","The basic structure is not responsible for inequalities in associations which one enters voluntarily":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Because an association which one enters into voluntarily is not a contingency of any kind (rather, it was a choice made by a rational agent), the basic structure does not provide protections against inequalities in such situations.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains Rawls’ views on voluntary associations.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An act is voluntary when an agent chooses to engage in it\">An act is voluntary when an agent chooses to engage in it</a><ul><li>A definition of voluntary, per Aristotle.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One cannot be treated unjustly voluntarily; one also cannot treat oneself unjustly\">One cannot be treated unjustly voluntarily; one also cannot treat oneself unjustly</a><ul><li>Again, Rawls shares this similarity with Aristotle.</li></ul></li></ul>","The laws and regulations of the EU often end up spreading outside its borders":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Known as the “Brussels Effect,” due to the geographic influence of the EU, its regulations and laws often spread outside its border. A prominent example of this is the GDPR, as many nations outside the EU have adopted its regulations in order to operate within the EU.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows the influence that European regulation can have, but also, that the negative effect that come from European regulation could spread around the world.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A growing gap between Europe and the US is caused by trade, religious, and political tensions\">A growing gap between Europe and the US is caused by trade, religious, and political tensions</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Legal regulation is not context sensitive\">Legal regulation is not context sensitive</a></li></ul>","We find something funny because we feel a temporary sense of superiority":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One of the foremost theories of humor states that we find something to be funny because we feel a temporary sense of superiority over the humorous thing.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This can be used to explain why we find something funny.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>When a comedian makes a joke about how disable people are not able to ambulate, the audience feels a temporary sense of superiority which makes them laugh.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We find something funny because it is a logical non-sequitur\">We find something funny because it is a logical non-sequitur</a><ul><li>Another theory.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We find something funny because it grants us relief\">We find something funny because it grants us relief</a><ul><li>Yet another theory.</li></ul></li></ul>","When two genes are farther apart, it is more likely that recombination will occur":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When two genes are farther apart on a chromosome, it is more likely that they are going to recombination with the paired sister chromatids.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why the recombination frequency increases as the distances between the genes increases.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Think of a chromosome like a long piece of rope. If you close your eyes and cut the rope at a random spot, you are much more likely to cut between two knots if they are at opposite ends of the rope than if they are right next to each other.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"During Prophase I, homologous chromosomes meet in a process called synapsis\">During Prophase I, homologous chromosomes meet in a process called synapsis</a><ul><li>The precondition. Recombination requires the tetrad that synapsis forms.</li></ul></li></ul>"});
